[38;2;124;124;124m$$d(f, g) = \int_{\mathbb{R}^n} \frac{|f(x) - g(x)|}{1 + |f(x) - g(x)|} \cdot [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\frac{dx}{(1+|x|)^n \cdot \text{something}}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the most standard topology on $\mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (measurable [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfunctions modulo a.e. equivalence) is the topology of local convergence in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasure. This is metrizable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait - the question is about continuous embedding. The key insight is:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $W^{k,2}(\mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathcal{M}$ to be a continuous embedding, we [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mneed the identity map from $C^\infty_c$ (with the $W^{k,2}$ norm) to $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{M}$ (with its topology) to extend continuously.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe topology of convergence in measure is weaker than any $L^p$ topology. So if [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$W^{k,2} \hookrightarrow L^p$ for some $p \geq 1$, then convergence in $L^p$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mimplies convergence in measure, so the map extends.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut actually, we need to think about what happens for all $k$.[0m

